# mubone firmware

The mubone firmware is an Arduino sketch. The main logic is seperated out into
template functions:

```c++
// @#'mubone_firmware.ino'
@{includes}

@{global defintions}

void setup()
{
    @{sensors setup}
}

void loop()
{
    OSCBundle error_messages;
    @{read sensors}
    @{receive OSC messages}
    @{dispatch OSC messages}
    @{transmit OSC messages}
}
// @
```

## OSC

All of the sensor readings are packed into an OSC bundle which is periodically
sent to the network via the attached ESP8266. The bundle is declared static
in the loop function and reused every loop so that memory only has to be
allocated for it once, the first time the loop runs. 

```c++
// @+'includes'
#include "OSCBundle.h"
// @

// @+'read sensors'
static OSCBundle bundle;
// @
```

## buttons

Buttons are the simplest sensors to measure. The internal pull-up resistor of
the MCU pin the button is attached to keeps the pin at a logical high voltage
until the button is pressed, connecting the pin directly to ground and
producing a logical low value.

```c++
// @+'global definitions'
struct Button
{
    const int pin;

    constexpr Button(const int p) : pin{p} {}

    void setup() const
    {
        pinMode(pin, INPUT_PULLUP);
    }

    void read(OSCBundle& bundle) const
    {
    }
}

constexpr Button buttons[8] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8};
// @

// @+'sensors setup'
for (const auto& button : buttons) button.setup();
// @

// @+'read sensors'
for (const auto& button: buttons) button.read(bundle);
// @
```

## joystick

The joystick button is read alongside all the other buttons. Its analog signals
are handled independently, but in a similarly simple manner using the Arduino
library to read from the MCU's ADC.

```c++
// @+'global definitions'
struct Joystick
{
    const int pin_x;
    const int pin_y;

    constexpr Joystick(const int x, const int y) : pin_x{x}, pin_y{y} {}

    void setup() const
    {
        pinMode(pin_x, INPUT);
        pinMode(pin_y, INPUT);
    }

    void read(OSCBundle& bundle) const
    {
        int x = analogRead(pin_x);
        int y = analogRead(pin_y);
    }
}

constexpr Joystick joystick{1, 2};
// @

// @+'sensors setup'
joystick.setup();
// @

// @+'read sensors'
joystick.read(bundle);
// @
```

## ADS1219 sensors

The slide position sensor, rotator, u-joint, and trigger use ADS1219 ADCs. Most
of the tricky parts of working with the ADC are handled by a seperate library.
The firmware has to keep track of which channel of the ADC is being read, and
has to manually cycle through the channels. The handling is very similar for
the slide position sensor (sps) and the sensors that share the other ADS1219.

```c++
// @+'includes'
#include "ADS1219.h"
// @

// @+'global definitions'
using namespace ADS1219;

Address sps_address(ADDR0::DGND, ADDR1::DGND);
ADS1219_ADC sps(sps_address, Wire);

Address misc_adc_address(AADR0::DGND. ADDR1::DGND);
ADS1219_ADC misc_adc(misc_adc_address, Wire);

struct ADS1219_Sensor
{
    const MUX channel;
    float reading;
    constexpr ADS1219_Sensor(const MUX c) : channel{c}, reading{0} {}
}

ADS1219_Sensor sps_pots[] = {MUX::AIN0_AIN1, MUX::AIN2_AIN3};
ADS1219_Sensor misc_pots[] = {MUX::AIN0, MUX::AIN1, MUX:AIN2, MUX::AIN3};

// @

// @+'sensors setup'
sps.set_config( MUX::AIN0_AIN1
              , RATE::SPS1000
              , MODE::CONTINUOUS
              , VREF::EXTERN
              );
sps.start_conversion();

misc_adc.set_config( MUX::AIN0
              , RATE::SPS1000
              , MODE::CONTINUOUS
              , VREF::EXTERN
              );
misc_adc.start_conversion();

// @

// @+'read sensors'
{
    static int pot = 0;
    if (sps.data_ready())
    {
        bool ret = sps.read_normalized(sps_pots[pot].reading);
        pot ^= 1; // switch to next potentiometer
        ret = sps.modify_config(sps_pots[pot].channel);
    }
}
{
    static int pot = 0;
    if (misc_adc.data_ready())
    {
        bool ret = sps.read_normalized(misc_pots[pot].rreading);
        pot = (pot + 1) & 0b11; // switch to next potentiometer
        ret = misc_adc.modify_config(misc_pots[pot].channel);
    }
}

// @
```

## MIMU

The MIMU sensors are particularly involved since they require calibration and
sensor fusion. Most of the details of these algorithms are abstracted away in
the MIMU libraries.

```c++
// @+'includes'
#include "MIMU.h"
// @

// @+'global definitions'
MIMU mimu(backend, address, Wire);
MIMUCalibrator mimu_calibrator{};
MIMUFusion mimu_fusion{};
// @

// @+'sensors setup'

// @

// @+'read sensors'
{
    static MIMUReading mimu_reading{};
    static Quaternion orientation = Quaternion::Zero;
    mimu.read(mimu_reading);
    mimu_calibrator.calibrate(mimu_reading);
    orientation = mimu_fusion.fuse(mimu_reading);
}
// @
```

